# N.I.N.A. Polaris - keep desktop camera handlers off DSLR bodies.
#
# libgphoto2 ships rules that tag every camera it knows with
# ID_GPHOTO2="1". The desktop's gphoto volume monitor watches udev for that
# tag and claims the PTP interface the instant a body is plugged in, so
# indi_gphoto_ccd then gets:
#
#   Camera open error (-53): Could not claim the USB device
#   Can not open camera: Power OK? If camera is auto-mounted as external
#   disk storage, please unmount it and disable auto-mount.
#
# Reported 2026-09-20 with a Canon EOS 1500D on the Raspberry Pi 4 image:
# the camera appears in lsusb and in the driver list, and every connect
# attempt fails with no explanation in the UI.
#
# Clearing the tag (this file sorts after libgphoto2's own rules, and
# UDISKS_IGNORE keeps the disk side away too) makes the camera invisible to
# the desktop handlers while leaving it fully available to libgphoto2
# itself: indi_gphoto enumerates over libusb and never consults udev.
#
# To restore normal desktop behaviour on this host, delete this file and run
# 'sudo udevadm control --reload-rules'.
ACTION=="add|change", ENV{ID_GPHOTO2}=="1", ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}="1", ENV{GPHOTO2_DRIVER}="", ENV{ID_GPHOTO2}=""
